<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Traditional Style of Dress</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is Wearing a Pollera.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Demonstrate comprehension of simple written exchanges in letters, emails, or social media. reading2: Write short responses to questions about a text. phonemicAwareness: Blend spoken simple onsets and rhymes to form real words (onset /c/ and rhyme /at/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can demonstrate comprehension, in short exchanges, by identifying words for traditional clothing (e.g., 'pollera,' 'dress.'). Can answer questions, in a text, about traditional clothing (e.g., 'What is a pollera?' 'Panama's traditional dress.'). Can blend spoken simple onsets and rhymes to read and spell one-syllable, two-syllable, and simple multisyllabic words related to cultural clothing (e.g., /dr/ /ɛs/ in 'dress' for one syllable, /ɪ/ /vɛnt/ in 'event' for two syllables, and /trə/ /dɪʃ/ /ən/ /əl/ in 'traditional' for multisyllabic words).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of traditional Panamanian clothing, especially the pollera, and other traditional dresses from different cultures. Students identify clothing items they know. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" or "What is this dress called?" to activate prior knowledge of vocabulary from Lesson 1 like 'dress,' 'traditional clothing,' 'pretty,' 'beautiful.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a large picture of a woman wearing a pollera. The teacher points to the pollera and says slowly and clearly, "She is wearing a beautiful pollera. It is Panama's traditional dress." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'wearing' (/w/) and 'these' (as in "These dresses are special"). The teacher writes 'pollera' and 'dress' on the board. The teacher models blending sounds: /dr/ /ɛs/ to form 'dress' and /pr/ /ɪt/ /i/ to form 'pretty.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is she wearing pants? (No, she is wearing a dress/pollera.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'pollera' a modern dress or a traditional dress? (It's a traditional dress.)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Traditional Style of Dress".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple descriptive text about the pollera, presented as a short paragraph on a worksheet or projected on a screen. The text uses vocabulary such as 'pollera,' 'traditional clothing,' 'beautiful,' 'festive,' 'event,' 'wear,' 'jewelry.' It also includes present continuous ('She is wearing...') and present simple ('Women wear this dress at festivals.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will demonstrate comprehension of a simple written text about traditional clothing by identifying key words and details.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read the text silently. The teacher asks, "What is this text about?" (Traditional Panamanian dress / the pollera).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read statements and circle 'True' or 'False' based on the text.</li>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pollera is a modern dress." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Women wear the pollera at festivals." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The pollera has no jewelry." (False)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students match words from the text (e.g., 'pollera', 'jewelry', 'event') with corresponding pictures.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading and identifying key information about traditional clothing using Present continuous ('She is wearing...') and Present simple ('Women wear...') with vocabulary: 'attire', 'celebration', 'event', 'community', 'traditional clothing', 'jewelry', 'showcase', 'represent', 'explain', 'admire', 'dress', 'use', 'wear', 'festive', 'pretty', 'beautiful', 'cultural', 'important', 'traditional', 'during', 'at', 'in.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the original text (or similar sentences) that have blanks. They choose from a word bank (e.g., 'pollera', 'wearing', 'festivals', 'jewelry', 'traditional') to complete the sentences. Example: "She is _______ a beautiful _______. Women wear this dress at _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Scramble:</b> Students are given scrambled words to form correct sentences related to the text. Example: "is / pollera / a / She / wearing / beautiful." → "She is wearing a beautiful pollera." This reinforces sentence structure and reading.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Phonemic Awareness & Reading:</b> The teacher says target words from the text, breaking them into onset and rhyme (e.g., /dr/ - /ɛs/ for 'dress'; /pr/ - /ɪt/ - /i/ for 'pretty'). Students blend the sounds to say the word and then circle or underline the word in a provided list. Teacher also practices /c/ - /at/ for 'cat' as a familiar example. Students then identify and read other one-syllable words from the text (e.g., 'wear', 'use', 'fold').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Pair Reading and Check:</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A reads a sentence from the gap-fill or scrambled sentences, and Partner B checks if it's correct according to the original text. They take turns reading and checking.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Traditional Style of Dress".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Reading and Written Response to simple social media posts or short descriptive cards.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive three different "Traditional Clothing Cards" or "Social Media Posts." Each card/post shows a picture of a person wearing a specific traditional Panamanian outfit (e.g., a pollera, a montuno, a devil's mask costume) and a very short, simple description (2-3 sentences) using target vocabulary and grammar. For example: "This is a Montuno. He is wearing a white shirt and a hat. People wear this traditional attire at special events."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one card and read the description silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After reading, they answer two simple WH-questions about the text on their chosen card on a separate answer sheet. Example questions: "What is she wearing?" (for a pollera card) or "When do people wear this attire?" (for a montuno card).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can then share their chosen card and answers with a partner, taking turns to *read* the description and *their written answers*.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension, identifying specific details, and writing short, accurate responses using target vocabulary and grammar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production stage, focusing on students' ability to read and understand the texts, and the accuracy of their written responses. Collection of student answer sheets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a new short, simple email or message (e.g., from a friend describing a traditional festival they attended). They read the email and then answer 2-3 WH-questions about the information presented. Example email snippet: "Hi [Student's Name]! Last weekend, I went to a community celebration. Many women were wearing beautiful polleras. They looked very festive! It was a great event."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension:</b> Correctly answers 2 out of 3 questions about the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Identifies and uses target vocabulary (e.g., 'pollera', 'celebration', 'festive', 'wearing') in answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Written responses are clear and easy to understand.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to read about _________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned is _________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about traditional clothing was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was a little difficult, and how can I practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the texts, comprehension of specific details, blending phonics activity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction)? (e.g., more visual support for new vocabulary, explicit pre-teaching of question forms for speaking, more peer-to-peer reading activities).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This reading lesson provided the content and vocabulary for students to understand descriptions of traditional clothing. The next lesson will focus on actively *speaking* and *interacting* about these traditional clothes, using the knowledge gained from reading. Students will use the grammar (Present continuous, WH-questions) and vocabulary to describe what people are wearing and ask others about it.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>